## 🔴 Critical Gaps

### 1. Missing Circuit Breaker & Fallback Strategy (Section 8)

> [!CAUTION]
> The architecture has no defined behavior when the ML endpoint, Redis, or downstream services are **unavailable**. In a real payment flow, "no response" is worse than a wrong response — it blocks the transaction indefinitely.

**Add to Section 8.1/8.3:**

```markdown
### 8.4 Fallback & Degradation Strategy

| Failure mode | Fallback behavior |
|---|---|
| Online feature store (Redis) unreachable | Score using **transaction-level features only** (stateless subset), with a conservative score bias (+0.15 to final score) to compensate for missing behavioral signals |
| ML endpoint timeout (>100ms) or 5xx | Fall back to a **rules-only engine** (velocity + amount thresholds from a pre-cached rule set in the Azure Function's memory), returning a "degraded" flag so downstream case management treats it as lower confidence |
| Service Bus unavailable | Azure Function writes the event to a **local retry queue** (Azure Storage Queue as fallback); a separate reconciliation job replays once Service Bus recovers |
| Full scoring path down | **Default-approve** with a hard cap (e.g., transactions <₹5,000 only), flag all others for deferred review — this is a business decision that must be agreed with fraud-ops, not a technical default |

- Implement **circuit breakers** (e.g., Polly in the Azure Function, or Azure API Management circuit-breaker policy) on the Redis and ML endpoint calls with configurable trip thresholds.
- Every degraded decision must carry a `scoring_mode` field (`full | partial_features | rules_only | default_approve`) so downstream analytics can segment model performance by scoring quality.
```

