### 7. Autoencoder Retraining Cadence & Data Selection (Section 7)

> [!IMPORTANT]
> The document says the autoencoder is "retrained periodically on recent legitimate traffic" but doesn't specify cadence or the critical question: **how do you know a transaction is "legitimate" for retraining purposes?**

```markdown
#### Autoencoder & Isolation Forest retraining protocol

- **Cadence:** Weekly retraining, triggered by the drift monitor or on schedule 
  (whichever fires first). Not daily — the autoencoder's reconstruction threshold 
  is sensitive to distribution shifts, and daily retraining risks chasing noise.
- **"Legitimate" label for retraining:** Use transactions that were **approved by 
  the decision engine AND not subsequently charged back within a 14-day lookback 
  window**. This is inherently delayed information — the autoencoder always trains 
  on data that is at least 14 days old, which is acceptable because it's learning 
  the "normal" distribution, not chasing recent fraud patterns (that's the 
  supervised model's job).
- **Contamination guard:** Even with the chargeback filter, assume up to 0.1% of 
  the "legitimate" training set is undetected fraud. Isolation Forest is robust to 
  this; for the autoencoder, use a **trimmed reconstruction loss** (discard the 
  top 1% of loss values during training) to prevent undetected fraud from 
  inflating the learned normality boundary.
```

